<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Research impact principles">
  <div><strong>Impact has stages</strong><span>Mechanistic insight, product development, human evidence, adoption and population outcomes are different milestones.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Context changes translation</strong><span>A result in a laboratory or athlete population does not automatically transfer to clinical care or humanitarian settings.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Implementation is measurable</strong><span>Reach, acceptability, feasibility, adoption, fidelity, cost and sustainability can matter as much as efficacy once a solution moves into the real world.</span></div>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Translation, not leapfrogging</p>
    <h2>Evidence should move through a chain, not jump straight to impact claims</h2>
    <p>The National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences describes translation as a spectrum in which basic, clinical, implementation and public-health stages inform one another. The stages are not perfectly linear, but they still require evidence appropriate to the question being asked.</p>
    <p>For Best Hydrate, that means established hydration physiology can inform formulation and study design, but broader impact claims require evidence from the relevant product, population, delivery system and setting.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Impact pathway</p>
  <h2>Five levels of useful progress</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__steps">
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Understand the mechanism</h3><p>Clarify fluid balance, intestinal transport, electrolytes, formulation chemistry and the limits of established physiology.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Build and characterize the product</h3><p>Define the formulation, preparation, concentration, sensory properties, stability and intended use conditions.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Generate product-specific evidence</h3><p>Test the actual formulation under protocols that match the claim, population and use case of interest.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Study real-world implementation</h3><p>Evaluate whether people can prepare, access, tolerate, adopt and use the solution consistently in the intended setting.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Measure outcomes that matter</h3><p>Only after the preceding stages are credible should health, performance, access, cost or population-level outcomes be described as impact.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__split">
  <div class="bhd-page__split-media">
    <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/6646924/pexels-photo-6646924.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Volunteers distributing bottled water and supplies at a community outreach event" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
  </div>
  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Implementation science</p>
    <h2>A solution can be effective and still fail in practice</h2>
    <p>Implementation research asks what happens after an intervention leaves the controlled study environment. Can people access it? Is preparation practical? Do users and organizations accept it? Can the process be delivered consistently? Is the cost sustainable?</p>
    <p>These questions are especially important in clinical and humanitarian settings, where workflow, water quality, storage, training, procurement, culture and local resources can determine whether a theoretically useful intervention produces any real-world benefit.</p>
    <p>That is why we treat implementation as a research problem of its own rather than assuming that efficacy automatically produces impact.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Three translation pathways</p>
  <h2>Impact means something different in each Best Hydrate segment</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article>
      <h3>Performance</h3>
      <p>Meaningful impact could include better individualized hydration planning, practical use during training and travel, acceptable gastrointestinal tolerance, and performance-supporting routines that are validated in the relevant athletes and conditions.</p>
      <a href="/pages/performance-hydration">Performance hydration →</a>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Clinical</h3>
      <p>Meaningful impact requires clinically relevant populations, endpoints, governance and care integration. A general hydration mechanism is not enough to establish a clinical indication or standard-of-care role.</p>
      <a href="/pages/clinical-development">Clinical development →</a>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Humanitarian</h3>
      <p>Meaningful impact can include preparation reliability, supply resilience, local acceptability, affordability, water access, training burden and appropriate distinction from WHO therapeutic ORS.</p>
      <a href="/pages/eco-humanitarian">Humanitarian hydration →</a>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">What we would measure</p>
    <h2>Impact should be tied to observable outcomes</h2>
    <p>Depending on the project, implementation outcomes can include <strong>acceptability, reach, adoption, appropriateness, feasibility, fidelity, cost and sustainability</strong>. These do not replace physiological or clinical outcomes, but they help explain whether a useful intervention can actually function in its intended environment.</p>
    <p>For a hydration product, additional practical measures may include preparation accuracy, adherence, palatability, gastrointestinal tolerance, distribution reliability and whether the correct population is receiving the correct formulation for the intended purpose.</p>
    <p>These measures are especially valuable when two interventions have similar biological rationale but very different usability or delivery constraints.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Impact rule</span>
    <h3>Do not count activity as outcome.</h3>
    <p>Samples distributed, meetings held or partnerships announced may describe effort. They are not evidence of improved hydration, health or performance unless outcomes are actually measured.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Responsible impact reporting</p>
  <h2>What we will separate in public reporting</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Inputs</h3><p>Funding, product, staff time, laboratory capacity, training and partner resources.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Activities</h3><p>Protocols, sampling, education, distribution, field visits, data collection and implementation support.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Outputs</h3><p>Participants reached, samples analyzed, protocols completed, publications, training sessions or products delivered.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Outcomes</h3><p>Measured changes in hydration, clinical endpoints, performance, access, adoption, cost or other predefined impact measures.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Humanitarian boundary</p>
    <h2>Access work must preserve the distinction between hydration products and therapeutic ORS</h2>
    <p>WHO reduced-osmolarity oral rehydration salts are a defined therapeutic formulation for diarrhoeal dehydration. Humanitarian research involving other hydration products must be explicit about intended use, formulation, preparation, evidence and referral pathways so that consumer hydration, supportive hydration and therapeutic ORS are not conflated.</p>
    <p>This is both a scientific and implementation issue. The correct product used for the wrong purpose is not successful translation.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__brand-context">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Our impact standard</p>
    <h2>Claim only the stage we have actually reached</h2>
    <p>We want research to influence product design, evidence priorities and real-world access. We also want the public record to make clear whether a statement describes rationale, a study objective, a measured finding or a demonstrated outcome.</p>
  </div>
